"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Introductory Statistics 2e</em>, §§{#} (The Central Limit Theorem for Sample Means), {#} (The Central Limit Theorem for Sums), {#} (Using the Central Limit Theorem) and {#} (Central Limit Theorem, Pocket Change). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/introductory-statistics-2e.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Simple random: pocket change; Stratified: {#} from each section; Cluster: two blocks of seats; Systematic: every 20th seat; Convenience: the ones by the door; Self selected: who chooses the videos?; Randomized: a coin decides; Means of skewed reaction times, n = {#}; Pocket change: sums of n = {#}; Uniform seconds: n = {#} makes a triangle; Bimodal commutes: n = {#}; n = {#}: the means are the population.",
"This worksheet uses the <strong>Central limit theorem</strong> view. The hall at the left holds the population, {#} students; the histogram at the right is the whole population, with its mean μ and standard deviation σ. The population here is <strong>Reaction times (ms), skewed</strong>: a simulated ruler drop reaction time for each student, {#} ms plus an exponential wait, so most are near {#} ms and a few are much slower.",
"Each sample draws n students <strong>with replacement</strong> (a student can be drawn twice), so the draws are independent. Type n in <strong>Sample size n</strong> (or use −{#} and +{#}); a new n starts a new set of samples.",
"<strong>Draw a sample</strong> draws one sample and rings its students; <strong>Draw {#}</strong> and <strong>Draw {#}</strong> draw that many at once; <strong>Play</strong> draws them one at a time, then faster, up to the next {#}. The <strong>Means | Sums</strong> switch chooses whether the lower histogram (and the readouts) show the sample means x̄ or the sample sums ΣX.",
"The lower graph is the histogram of the means (or sums) with the normal curve the theorem predicts (teal) and its center (green). At the right, <strong>Spread of the means against n</strong> puts a dot at each n you have drawn {#} or more samples for, under the curve σ/√n.",
"The Readouts give <strong>Population μ</strong> and <strong>Population σ</strong>, <strong>Mean of the x̄’s</strong>, <strong>Spread, s of the x̄’s</strong>, <strong>Theorem: σ/√n</strong>, <strong>Skew of these</strong> (the skewness of the means drawn), <strong>Theorem: skew of population/√n</strong>, and the share of the means <strong>Within ±{#} standard error</strong> and <strong>Within ±{#} standard errors</strong> of μ. With Sums chosen the same readouts are for the sums.",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> stores n, {#}/√n, √n and these readouts; <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> fills Table {#} (means) or Table {#} (sums); <strong>Clear</strong> empties the Readings list.",
"Everything is simulated by a seeded random number generator (draw set {#} in the Lab section): the same n gives the same samples every time. No real reaction times were measured.",
"Draw thousands of samples from a skewed population and measure how the spread and the shape of the sample mean change with n: check that the spread is σ/√n, find the n where a normal curve describes the means well, and do the same for sums.",
"Draw a sample of n values from a population with mean μ and standard deviation σ, and compute its mean x̄. Another sample gives another x̄. The <strong>sampling distribution of x̄</strong> is the distribution of all those x̄’s. The <strong>central limit theorem</strong> (§{#}) says that for independent draws:",
"the mean of the x̄’s is μ (x̄ is unbiased);",
"the standard deviation of the x̄’s is \\(\\sigma_{\\bar x} = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}\\), the <strong>standard error of the mean</strong>;",
"for large enough n, the x̄’s follow a normal distribution, whatever the shape of the population.",
"The first two hold for every n; only the shape needs n to be large. The same holds for the <strong>sum</strong> of the sample, ΣX = n x̄ (§{#}): its mean is nμ and its standard deviation is \\(\\sqrt n\\,\\sigma\\). A sum spreads more as n grows; a mean spreads less.",
"How large is large enough? The book’s rule of thumb is n of {#} or more, but it depends on the population. One measure of shape is the <strong>skewness</strong>: {#} for a symmetric distribution, positive for a long right tail. For independent draws the skewness of x̄ is the population’s skewness divided by √n, so a skewed population needs a larger n. A normal distribution also has {#} % of its values within one standard deviation of its center and {#} % within two; you can check those shares, too.",
"Physics connection: averaging n repeated measurements cuts the random error by √n, which is why an average of {#} readings is ten times better than one. And a measurement that is the sum of many small independent effects (a timing error, the noise in a sensor) tends to be normal, which is why the normal curve is everywhere in the lab.",
"To use the theorem (§{#}), convert to z with the standard error: \\(z = \\frac{\\bar x - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\).",
